Come join us at aiprofitboardroom.com.

1855
02:21:54,030 --> 02:21:57,444
Thanks for watching, and I'll see you in the next one. Remember Notebook LM?

1856
02:21:57,616 --> 02:22:00,336
That tool everyone used to make AI podcasts from their notes.

1857
02:22:00,526 --> 02:22:08,324
Google just gave it a brand new name. It's called Gemini Notebook now, and it does way more than summarize your files. Most people have no idea it can build and run things on its own.

1858
02:22:08,558 --> 02:22:16,484
I'm the digital avatar of Julian Goldie, and I help people actually learn and use AI tools in their real work, not just watch videos about them.

1859
02:22:16,656 --> 02:22:20,416
In this one, I'm going to walk you through Gemini Notebook from the very start.

1860
02:22:20,622 --> 02:22:24,416
What it is, how it got here, every part of the screen, and how to set it up the right way.

1861
02:22:24,496 --> 02:22:33,348
And near the end, I'll show you the one new upgrade that turns this from a simple note tool into something a lot more powerful. Stick around for that one because almost nobody's talking about it yet.

1862
02:22:33,616 --> 02:22:36,416
So let's start simple. What is Gemini Notebook?

1863
02:22:36,622 --> 02:22:38,816
Think of it as your own AI research partner.

1864
02:22:39,022 --> 02:22:46,496
You give it your sources, your documents, your PDFs, your Google Docs, a website, even a YouTube video, and then you can ask it questions about all of it.

1865
02:22:46,576 --> 02:22:52,016
The big thing that makes it special, it only answers from the stuff you gave it. It doesn't wander off and make things up.

1866
02:22:52,256 --> 02:22:56,576
Every answer points right back to your sources, so you can always check where it came from.

1867
02:22:56,782 --> 02:23:00,816
That's huge if you care about getting things right instead of getting a confident guess.

1868
02:23:01,102 --> 02:23:02,656
Now here's the part a lot of people missed.

1869
02:23:02,990 --> 02:23:04,960
This tool didn't just show up out of nowhere.

1870
02:23:05,166 --> 02:23:10,596
It started back at Google's big event in 2023 under a code name Project Tailwind.

1871
02:23:10,734 --> 02:23:13,120
The whole goal was simple, help people learn.

1872
02:23:13,280 --> 02:23:21,680
Then Google gave it the name Notebook LM, and it kind of blew up. Today, over 30 million people use it. More than 600,000 organizations too.

1873
02:23:21,840 --> 02:23:28,160
Students turning notes into study guides, business owners building training material, writers, researchers, all of them.

1874
02:23:28,320 --> 02:23:34,240
Then in July of 2026, Google renamed it one more time. Notebook LM became Gemini Notebook.

1875
02:23:34,400 --> 02:23:39,600
Same tool people already loved, just tied much more tightly into the rest of Google's AI world.

1876
02:23:39,760 --> 02:23:42,160
And that name change came with real muscle.

1877
02:23:42,382 --> 02:23:45,680
Gemini Notebook can now build and automate way more than before.

1878
02:23:45,840 --> 02:23:53,988
It doesn't just chat with your files, it can turn them into audio shows, into videos, slide decks, mind maps, study guides, you name it.

1879
02:23:54,126 --> 02:23:55,876
And the newest upgrade is the big one.

1880
02:23:56,080 --> 02:23:59,760
Every notebook now gets its own secure little cloud computer.

1881
02:23:59,982 --> 02:24:09,188
That means it can actually write and run code for you right inside the notebook to dig into your data. I'll come back to that because it's the piece most people haven't caught on to yet.

1882
02:24:09,390 --> 02:24:10,640
Okay, so how do you get in?

1883
02:24:10,800 --> 02:24:11,200
Easy.

1884
02:24:11,360 --> 02:24:15,440
Just head to notebook.google.com, sign in with any Google account, and you're in.

1885
02:24:15,600 --> 02:24:21,572
No download needed. It works right in your web browser and there are apps for your phone too, both iPhone and Android.

1886
02:24:21,760 --> 02:24:25,280
When you first land inside, you'll notice some notebooks already sitting there.

1887
02:24:25,486 --> 02:24:34,720
These are ones Google made or ones from big publications, just to show you what's possible. Click around them for a minute. It's a really nice way to see the tool in action before you build your own.

1888
02:24:34,800 --> 02:24:42,640
But don't stop there because the real magic happens when you make one from scratch with your own stuff. Now let me show you the layout because once you get this, the whole thing just clicks.

1889
02:24:42,894 --> 02:24:45,680
When you open a notebook, the screen splits into three parts.

1890
02:24:45,902 --> 02:24:50,640
On the left you've got sources, in the middle you've got chat, and on the right, you've got the studio panel.

1891
02:24:50,800 --> 02:24:53,760
Let me break down each one because each does a completely different job.

1892
02:24:53,920 --> 02:24:55,120
First, sources.

1893
02:24:55,280 --> 02:24:58,240
This is on the left, and honestly, this is the heart of everything.

1894
02:24:58,480 --> 02:25:09,600
Sources are the files and links you feed the tool. You can add PDFs, Google Docs, Google Slides, websites, YouTube videos, audio files, even images. Just click add and drop them in.

1895
02:25:09,680 --> 02:25:19,716
You can paste in raw text too or copy a link straight in and you can stack a whole pile of sources into a single notebook. So this becomes like one big brain that knows everything you fed it.

1896
02:25:19,840 --> 02:25:22,080
Now here's a detail most people never notice.

1897
02:25:22,240 --> 02:25:32,836
Every source gets its own little checkbox. That means you can turn sources on and off whenever you want. So if you only want answers from two specific documents right now, you just uncheck the rest.

1898
02:25:33,006 --> 02:25:34,720
And the tool only looks at those two.

1899
02:25:34,880 --> 02:25:37,520
That's a simple trick that keeps your answers really focused.

1900
02:25:37,774 --> 02:25:44,160
And when you click on any source, Gemini Notebook shows you a quick summary of what's inside it plus the key topics it spotted.

1901
02:25:44,334 --> 02:25:48,932
So you can understand a long document without even reading the whole thing. And it's smart about what it reads.

1902
02:25:49,262 --> 02:25:54,880
Drop in a YouTube video and it works off the transcript so you can get through an hour-long video in seconds.

1903
02:25:55,118 --> 02:26:03,840
Add an audio file and it transcribes that too. You can also rename any source to keep things tidy, so a messy file name turns into something you'll actually recognize later.

1904
02:26:04,110 --> 02:26:07,280
Little things like that keep a big notebook from turning into a mess.

1905
02:26:07,440 --> 02:26:11,652
Then there's a discover option, and this one's a gem a lot of folks skip right past.

1906
02:26:11,854 --> 02:26:21,280
Instead of hunting for sources yourself, you type in a question, and Gemini Notebook goes and finds real sources on the web for you, each one with a citation, so you know where it came from.

1907
02:26:21,486 --> 02:26:24,720
You look at what it found, pick the good ones, and pull them in with a click.

1908
02:26:24,974 --> 02:26:29,040
So even if you're staring at a blank notebook with nothing to add, you're never really stuck.

1909
02:26:29,326 --> 02:26:31,200
Next up, the middle, that's chat.

1910
02:26:31,406 --> 02:26:37,520
This is where you actually talk to your notebook. You ask it anything about your sources and it answers, but only using what you gave it.

1911
02:26:37,680 --> 02:26:38,532
Ask for a summary.

1912
02:26:38,720 --> 02:26:40,880
Ask it to explain a hard part in plain words.

1913
02:26:41,070 --> 02:26:46,080
Ask it to compare two documents side by side. Ask it to pull out every action item, whatever you need.

1914
02:26:46,286 --> 02:26:54,240
And to get you started, it even suggests a few questions you can just tap on so you're never sitting there wondering what to ask first. Now, here's the part that really matters.

1915
02:26:54,400 --> 02:26:58,720
Every single answer comes with little citations. These small numbers you can click.

1916
02:26:58,800 --> 02:27:01,680
And when you click one, it jumps you straight to the exact spot.

1917
02:27:02,176 --> 02:27:04,016
In the exact source the answer came from.

1918
02:27:04,238 --> 02:27:07,296
So you're never taking its word for it. You can see the proof in one click.

1919
02:27:07,536 --> 02:27:09,616
No guessing, no made-up nonsense.

1920
02:27:09,776 --> 02:27:13,536
That grounding is the whole reason people trust this thing for real work.

1921
02:27:13,774 --> 02:27:17,856
And when you get an answer you love, you can save it as a note so it's parked right there for later.

1922
02:27:18,030 --> 02:27:20,256
You can even tell the chat how you want it to talk to you.

1923
02:27:20,462 --> 02:27:28,096
Ask it to keep answers short and simple, or to go long and detailed, or to explain things like you're brand new to the topic. It will shape itself around how you learn.

1924
02:27:28,206 --> 02:27:29,856
And the chat remembers where you are.

1925
02:27:30,016 --> 02:27:38,576
Ask a follow-up and it builds on the last answer so you can keep digging without repeating yourself. And it's not just for questions either. You can ask it to actually write things for you.

1926
02:27:38,766 --> 02:27:46,404
Draft an email, build an outline, pull together a summary, all grounded in your sources, so it never just makes stuff up. Quick question before we move on.

1927
02:27:46,638 --> 02:27:50,724
Can you guess which panel does the heavy lifting for actually building content?

1928
02:27:50,896 --> 02:27:52,708
Keep watching because it's this next one.

1929
02:27:52,942 --> 02:27:54,816
And that brings us to the start of the show.

1930
02:27:55,054 --> 02:27:56,816
The studio panel over on the right.

1931
02:27:56,976 --> 02:28:01,376
This is where your boring pile of documents turns into something you'd actually want to use.

1932
02:28:01,582 --> 02:28:05,776
With one click, Studio can spin your sources into an audio overview.

1933
02:28:05,936 --> 02:28:13,536
That's the famous one where two AI voices chat about your content like a real podcast, breaking it all down in a way that's easy to follow.

1934
02:28:13,774 --> 02:28:14,736
And it got even better.

1935
02:28:14,926 --> 02:28:17,856
There's an interactive mode where you can actually join the conversation.

1936
02:28:18,062 --> 02:28:24,576
You hit a button and you can talk to the hosts and ask some questions live right in the middle of the show, and they answer using your sources.

1937
02:28:24,782 --> 02:28:27,616
You can also pick different languages so you're not locked to English.

1938
02:28:27,726 --> 02:28:28,836
But that's just the start.

1939
02:28:29,056 --> 02:28:37,376
You can make a video overview where it narrates your content with visuals pulled straight from your sources, even a short vertical video like the kind you post on your phone.

1940
02:28:37,582 --> 02:28:42,416
You can make mind maps that lay your ideas out visually so you see how everything connects.

1941
02:28:42,576 --> 02:28:51,216
Reports like study guides, briefings, FAQs and timelines, flashcards and quizzes to help you actually learn the material, infographics,

1942
02:28:51,376 --> 02:28:53,456
even slide decks and data tables.

1943
02:28:53,678 --> 02:28:56,164
And a lot of these you can actually use, not just look at.

1944
02:28:56,398 --> 02:29:05,028
The flashcards and quizzes are interactive, so you flip through them and test yourself and it checks your answers. Everything you make gets saved right there in the panel.

1945
02:29:05,166 --> 02:29:06,736
So you can come back to it anytime.

1946
02:29:06,896 --> 02:29:12,484
You can even peek at the prompt it used to build something, which is a great way to learn how to steer it yourself. And here's a tip.

1947
02:29:12,878 --> 02:29:15,936
Before you hit generate, you can usually customize it first.

1948
02:29:16,142 --> 02:29:23,536
There's a little option to steer it, so you can tell it what to focus on or pick the format and language you want instead of just taking whatever it gives you.

1949
02:29:23,790 --> 02:29:30,176
Then once it's built, you can share it or send a report straight into Google Docs and a data table straight into Google Sheets.

1950
02:29:30,336 --> 02:29:35,776
All of it built from your sources, all of it grounded with citations, and all of it in basically one click.

1951
02:29:35,950 --> 02:29:39,056
This right here is the build and automate part everyone keeps talking about.

1952
02:29:39,310 --> 02:29:42,736
Now you might be thinking, why not just ask a normal AI chatbot instead?

1953
02:29:42,896 --> 02:29:43,536
Great question.

1954
02:29:43,790 --> 02:29:45,456
And here's the difference that really matters.

1955
02:29:45,678 --> 02:29:50,084
When you ask a regular chatbot something, it pulls from everything it ever learned.

1956
02:29:50,222 --> 02:29:57,067
And sometimes it just makes things up and says them with total confidence. Gemini Notebook doesn't do that. it's locked to your sources.

1957
02:29:57,486 --> 02:30:02,576
It will only answer from the files and links you gave it, and it shows you exactly where each answer came from.

1958
02:30:02,798 --> 02:30:08,496
So if you're working with your own research, your own documents, your own notes, you get answers you can actually trust and check.

1959
02:30:08,750 --> 02:30:14,016
That's the whole point of it. It trades knowing a little about everything for knowing your stuff deeply and honestly.

1960
02:30:14,254 --> 02:30:16,336
For real work, that trade is worth a lot.

1961
02:30:16,622 --> 02:30:22,336
Now, before we build one, let me show you a few things you can actually do with all this because this is where it gets really handy.

1962
02:30:22,542 --> 02:30:30,736
Take that audio overview. You're not stuck with one version. You can make it shorter if you just want the quick rundown, or longer if you want it to really go deep.

1963
02:30:30,816 --> 02:30:33,296
You can speed it up or slow it down while you listen.

1964
02:30:33,486 --> 02:30:41,056
And you can download it so you can pop it on your phone and listen to your own notes like a podcast while you're out on a walk. Same idea with the other outputs.

1965
02:30:41,136 --> 02:30:43,296
That mind map isn't just a pretty picture.

1966
02:30:43,406 --> 02:30:51,696
You can click any branch to open it up and you can click a point and turn it straight into a question so you dig deeper into just that one idea instead of the whole thing at once.

1967
02:30:51,856 --> 02:30:54,416
Then there's sharing, and this is a big one people forget about.

1968
02:30:54,576 --> 02:30:56,976
Any notebook you build, you can share with someone else.

1969
02:30:57,166 --> 02:31:07,012
Send a teammate a link and they can open it, read through it, and ask their own questions against the same sources. So this stops being a solo tool and becomes something a whole team can use together.

1970
02:31:07,278 --> 02:31:12,656
It's great for onboarding new people, group projects, or just getting everyone on the same page fast.

1971
02:31:13,008 --> 02:31:15,408
Without you having to explain the same thing 10 times.

1972
02:31:15,630 --> 02:31:17,248
And here's the best part for most people.

1973
02:31:17,422 --> 02:31:18,848
You can do all of this for free.

1974
02:31:18,958 --> 02:31:20,288
You just sign in and start.

1975
02:31:20,494 --> 02:31:27,168
There are higher limits on the upgraded plans if you're a heavy user, but the free version is plenty for most folks to get real work done.

1976
02:31:27,342 --> 02:31:34,848
So don't let that stop you. Just jump in and start building. Speaking of which, let's do exactly that. Alright, let's actually build one because this is where it gets fun.

1977
02:31:35,022 --> 02:31:41,968
Back on the main page, you just click new notebook, give it a clear name, something like AI research or client project, whatever you're working on.

1978
02:31:42,128 --> 02:31:46,128
Then you head over to that sources panel on the left and you start adding your stuff.

1979
02:31:46,318 --> 02:31:55,648
Drop in a few PDFs, paste a website link, add a YouTube video, or use that Discover button we talked about and let it go find sources for you. Here's my one big tip on this.

1980
02:31:55,888 --> 02:31:59,408
The quality of everything you get out depends completely on what you put in.

1981
02:31:59,630 --> 02:32:05,508
Feed it good, focused sources, and your answers, your audio, your reports, all of it comes out sharp.

1982
02:32:05,774 --> 02:32:10,128
Feed it junk, and you get junk back. So spend an extra minute picking the right sources.

1983
02:32:10,350 --> 02:32:12,288
It matters more than anything else you'll do.

1984
02:32:12,462 --> 02:32:14,448
So what do people actually build with this?

1985
02:32:14,702 --> 02:32:16,932
Let me give you a few ideas to get your wheels turning.

1986
02:32:17,168 --> 02:32:27,008
Students drop in their lecture notes, their textbook chapters, and a few articles, then turn the whole pile into a study guide, a set of flashcards, and a quiz to test themselves before an exam.

1987
02:32:27,214 --> 02:32:35,488
Researchers load up a stack of long PDFs and use the chat to pull out the key findings across all of them at once, instead of reading each one cover to cover.

1988
02:32:35,662 --> 02:32:45,008
If you run a team, you can drop in your onboarding docs and your process guides, then hand new people a notebook they can ask questions to so they're not stuck waiting on you for every little thing.

1989
02:32:45,262 --> 02:32:52,128
Writers and creators feed in their old posts, transcripts and notes, then spin out summaries, outlines, and audio versions of their own work.

1990
02:32:52,334 --> 02:32:58,368
Even for something simple like a dense report, you can drop it in and just ask in plain words, what does this actually say?

1991
02:32:58,606 --> 02:32:59,968
The pattern is always the same.

1992
02:33:00,128 --> 02:33:04,196
Gather your sources, then let the tool do the heavy lifting on top of them.

1993
02:33:04,462 --> 02:33:10,848
Now remember that upgrade I promised to come back to? Here it is. Every notebook now comes with its own secure cloud computer built right in.

1994
02:33:11,008 --> 02:33:19,328
In plain words, that means Gemini Notebook can write and run actual code for you right inside your notebook without you ever touching a single line of it.

1995
02:33:19,502 --> 02:33:22,528
Say you've got a messy spreadsheet full of numbers in your sources.

1996
02:33:22,688 --> 02:33:24,676
Before, the tool could talk about it.

1997
02:33:24,878 --> 02:33:30,128
Now it can actually crunch it, run the analysis, and hand you back real results grounded in your own data.

1998
02:33:30,350 --> 02:33:31,088
That's the leap.

1999
02:33:31,248 --> 02:33:35,168
It went from a tool that just reads your files to a tool that actually works with them.

2000
02:33:35,248 --> 02:33:39,008
And because it's running real code, it can go further than a quick answer.

2001
02:33:39,182 --> 02:33:47,648
It can spot trends across your numbers, build a chart from them, clean up a messy table, or pull the important figures out of a long report and lay them out clearly.

2002
02:33:47,854 --> 02:33:51,968
All of it's still tied to the data you gave it, so nothing is guessed or invented.

2003
02:33:52,128 --> 02:33:59,568
This is the part that turns Gemini Notebook from a research helper into something closer to a research assistant that can actually do the work.

2004
02:33:59,758 --> 02:34:08,768
This is rolling out step by step, starting with the top plans and reaching more people over the coming weeks. So if you don't see it in your account yet, don't worry, it's on the way.

2005
02:34:08,848 --> 02:34:10,768
A couple more things that make this really powerful.

2006
02:34:11,118 --> 02:34:16,688
Your notebooks now sync with the Gemini app, so the same sources and notes follow you across Google.

2007
02:34:16,910 --> 02:34:23,408
And Google's even bringing this into search. So your research isn't stuck in one little spot anymore, it travels with you wherever you work.

2008
02:34:23,598 --> 02:34:26,288
And speaking of taking it everywhere, don't sleep on the phone apps.

2009
02:34:26,510 --> 02:34:30,368
Gemini Notebook works right on your phone on both iPhone and Android.

2010
02:34:30,478 --> 02:34:35,088
So you can create a notebook, add sources, and chat with them while you're away from your desk.

2011
02:34:35,342 --> 02:34:37,408
But the best part on mobile is the audio.

2012
02:34:37,582 --> 02:34:46,448
You can play your audio overviews on the go, even in the background, so you can lock your phone, drop it in your pocket, and listen to your own notes turned into a podcast while you drive,

2013
02:34:46,638 --> 02:34:53,408
walk the dog, or do the dishes. It's a really easy way to finally get through your reading when you never seem to have time to sit down and read.

2014
02:34:53,646 --> 02:34:55,008
And here's another one people miss.

2015
02:34:55,342 --> 02:35:03,616
You're not limited to sharing a notebook with just one person. You can open a notebook up more widely so a whole group can view it and ask their own questions against the same sources.

2016
02:35:03,854 --> 02:35:09,456
Think of a class, a book club, or a whole department all working out of one shared, trusted set of documents.

2017
02:35:09,616 --> 02:35:14,976
That's when this really starts to feel less like a note tool and more like a shared brain everyone can tap into.

2018
